WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:07.540
Every day the perishable goods are packed
up for transport.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:16.900
Harvest Fresh supplies large supermarket chains
with vegetables grown on the company's own

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.060
fields and nearby farms.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.580
Driver Lucky Thantheni is preparing his cargo.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:32.580
Today he's taking it to a big warehouse in
Johannesburg.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:42.620
The truck interior has to reach the required temperature before the delicate freight can be loaded.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.200
Final instructions from the logistics manager.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:55.140
The vegetables have to be kept cool at a constant temperature of 2.7 degrees Celsius

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:56.180
during the transport.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:58.920
That will be checked on delivery.

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:04.300
"We need to make sure that everything is packed
correctly, is sealed correctly.

00:01:04.309 --> 00:01:11.020
Because any, let's say, any heat and stuff
that comes into the vegetables, that are not

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.840
sealed correctly, not cooled properly, goes
to waste."

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:19.700
Refrigerated transport is important in a hot
country like South Africa.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:23.760
This is one of the 13-thousand or so refrigerated
trucks in the country.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:28.180
The fleet emits enormous amounts of greenhouse
gases.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:34.619
The cargo area of most trucks is poorly insulated,
resulting in greater diesel fuel consumption

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.220
and spoiled produce.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:43.540
South Africa's agency for standards, the SABS, wants to make refrigerated transports more climate-friendly.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:50.240
A testing hall for refrigerated trucks was
recently opened here - the first on the African continent.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:56.620
Germany's agency for international development
cooperation, the GIZ, supported the construction

00:01:56.620 --> 00:02:01.880
of the hall by making contacts to companies
that operate similar testing facilities in Europe.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.700
The technology had to be adapted for use in
South Africa.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:16.100
Transport vehicles here are bigger and more
powerful than they are in Europe.

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:22.140
At the moment, only one out of five manufacturers
meets the minimum standards for insulation.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:28.600
For the first time ever, the new facility
allows testers here to precisely determine

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:31.380
just how well the trucks are insulated.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:34.180
There was no exact data available up to now.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:39.520
"That gives a tool to all the manufactorers
to improve their insulation properly.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:43.480
They can actually see their results in the
practical performance of the improvements

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:44.739
that they have made.

00:02:44.739 --> 00:02:47.669
So that they can implement that into production.

00:02:47.669 --> 00:02:52.950
With a lot of new trucks out there with better
insulation, we know we will use less diesel

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:54.580
to run the cooling systems.

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:58.840
Less diesel means less carbon emissions into
the atmosphere."

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:02.920
Every vehicle tested here gets an environmental
seal.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:08.440
Before long, the seal will be required on
all vehicles that transport fresh produce abroad.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:14.040
It tells how much fuel the vehicle needs for
refrigeration, and gives the values measured

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.800
by the temperature sensors in the testing
chamber.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:28.540
Entering Johannesburg.

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:34.900
Lucky Thantheni has been on the road with
his vegetable truck for about an hour-and-a-half.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:39.240
A special coolant helps maintain the low temperatures
in the cargo area.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:43.800
Most refrigerated trucks use this coolant,
which contains a chemical that is harmful

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:45.390
to the environment.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:49.500
Its impact on the atmosphere is 4-thousand
times greater than CO2.

00:03:55.910 --> 00:04:00.370
The Transfrig company plans on switching to
another refrigerant.

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:04.530
The company has developed this prototype that
will soon hit the market.

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:09.150
It says it's the world's only cooling system
that uses propane, while minimizing the risk

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:11.400
of using the flammable substance.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:18.960
"We use propane, because it is totally environmentally
friendly.

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:22.480
It does nothing to the OZONE layer.

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:25.100
Its carbon footprint is very small.

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:27.170
And that is why we chose propane.

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:29.160
And it's also very easily obtainable."

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:34.340
Using propane can sink coolant emissions by
a factor of a thousand.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:38.360
Transfrig tested its prototype in this truck.

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:43.660
In the summer, it transports ice cream, cooled
to minus 25-degrees Celsius.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:50.960
The company plans to produce five to ten further
propane-based cooling units here in the coming

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:55.700
months, and to test them on the road and put
the final touches on the new technology.

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:05.140
The cooling unit can be powered by a small
motor. That saves on fuel costs.

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:11.740
If everything works out as planned,

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:16.680
the conventional, environmentally harmful
coolant will no longer be used here.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:22.230
The company wants to install the propane technology
in all refrigeration units for large, medium

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:23.960
and small vehicles.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:32.460
"Then we will look at exporting to local African
markets - Zambia, Zimbabwe, Namibia, Botswana,

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:33.660
Mosambique.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:38.240
We have a very good presence in all these
markets.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:42.280
And once it's proven here we will it introduce
it into those markets."

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:49.400
Experts estimate that by 2030, South Africa
can reduce its CO2 emissions by 2 million

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:54.500
tons - alone through better insulation and
propane-based cooling units.

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:04.420
It's a first step toward making South Africa's
refrigerated transport climate-friendly.

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:08.840
And that is urgently needed to sustain the
sector's rapid growth.

